  "response": "## Where the \"who may export billing\" decision is made\n\n`crimes scan --format json` names the duplicate directly — `crime_00008`, `duplicated_policy` / \"Policy Doppelgänger\", severity **high**, confidence **0.88**, normalised rule `((\"admin\" === .role) && (\"free\" !== .plan))`. Reading the fixture confirms it and turns up three more places the same decision is encoded that the guard-clause detector can't merge into that finding.\n\n**The two live guard clauses (the actual policy):**\n\n1. `src/routes/export.ts:8` — `exportBilling(user, res)`\n   ```js\n   if (user.role === \"admin\" && user.plan !== \"free\") {\n     return res.send(await loadBillingExport(user.tenantId));\n   }\n   return res.status(403).end();\n   ```\n   The HTTP path. Re-exported from `src/index.ts:1`, so this is the package's public surface.\n\n2. `src/services/entitlements.ts:3` — `canExportBilling(member)`\n   ```js\n   if (member.role === \"admin\" && member.plan !== \"free\") {\n     return true;\n   }\n   return false;\n   ```\n   Commented \"Entitlement checks used by the worker path.\" Note: **nothing in this repo imports it.** Either the caller is out of tree or it's dead — and the PR author can't tell which from here.\n\nCrimes' evidence is explicit that nothing links them: *\"each copy is inlined in a different function (`canExportBilling`, `exportBilling`) rather than delegating to a shared one — no call site links them\"*, spanning two layers (routes, services).\n\n**Three more places the same decision is written down, as data rather than as a branch:**\n\n3. `src/contracts/user.ts:9` — `interface User { … role: \"admin\" | \"member\" … }`. The API's declared role vocabulary has no `owner`. Crucially this is *not* a tripwire: `exportBilling`'s params are untyped and the repo has no `tsconfig.json`, so widening the route will not produce a type error here. The contract silently goes stale.\n\n4. `src/repo/user-schema.ts:8` — `role: z.enum([\"admin\", \"member\", \"owner\"])`. Persistence already accepts owners. The PR is legalising a value the database layer has been storing all along.\n\n5. `src/repo/user-schema 2.ts:17` — `role: z.enum([\"admin\", \"member\"])`. The Finder conflict copy (`crime_00022`), stale, also exporting the symbol `UserSchema`. If any path parses through this one, an owner record is rejected at validation time — before it ever reaches the guard the PR edited. Two exported `UserSchema` bindings, nothing declaring which is canonical.\n\n**One adjacent rule worth naming even though it isn't this decision:** `src/services/entitlements.ts:10` — `canManageSeats`: `member.role === \"owner\" && member.plan !== \"free\"`. It's the only place `owner` appears in logic, so it's the nearest precedent an agent will copy, and after this PR it will look near-identical to the export rule. It is not the same rule and must not be merged into it.\n\n## If the PR changes only the site it was pointed at\n\nSay it's pointed at `routes/export.ts:8` (the likely target — it's the one with \"export\" in the path):\n\n- The HTTP route lets owners through. `canExportBilling` still returns `false` for them.\n- The divergence is **silent**. `canExportBilling` returns a bare boolean, not an error — so the worker path reads as \"this user has no entitlement,\" indistinguishable from \"this user has no data.\" Same user, allowed in the UI, empty in the batch path, nothing logged.\n- `contracts/user.ts` still says `\"admin\" | \"member\"`, so the contract now lies about the API's own role vocabulary, with no compiler complaining.\n- If the copy at `user-schema 2.ts` is on the path, owners fail validation upstream of the guard — the PR appears not to work at all, and the obvious next move (edit the guard again) won't fix it.\n\nPointed at `entitlements.ts` instead, it's worse in a quieter way: the route still 403s, so nothing user-visible changes, and the PR still reads as complete.\n\nThere is no test on either site. The repo's only test file is `src/services/payments.test.ts` — which matches `test_gap: 0.5` on this finding. Nothing fails either way.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**Block** — narrowly, and not for tidiness. This is an authorization widening where the same rule has two independently maintained copies, no test pins either, and the failure mode of getting it half-right is a silent inconsistency rather than an error. \"It works when I hit the endpoint\" is satisfiable by a wrong PR here.\n\nUnblock conditions, both in this PR:\n- Both sites move in one commit, or one delegates to the other.\n- `contracts/user.ts:9` gains `\"owner\"`, since the PR is changing what the API accepts.\n- One test per branch of the rule.\n\nI'd also require a decision on `user-schema 2.ts` before merge — deleting it is a one-line change and it's the difference between this PR working and mysteriously not working.\n\n## The single change that stops the next agent\n\nMake `entitlements.ts` the only place the rule exists, and have the route call it:\n\n```js\n// src/routes/export.ts\nif (!canExportBilling(user)) return res.status(403).end();\nreturn res.send(await loadBillingExport(user.tenantId));\n```\n\nThat's crimes' `extract_policy_function` action. Pick this over the test if you only get one: the test pins today's behaviour but still leaves two editable copies, so the next agent can make one of them fail and won't know which was authoritative. Collapsing to one function means the next widening — owners, then service accounts — is a single-site edit that cannot go half-applied.",
